body:
After looking through the source code I found that the problem is that when the scene is getting packed there's some code that checks if a variables value is already present in the `PackedScene` by checking if the variable's hash is present in the HashMap. If it is then it will link both the variable names to the same value. 

The problem here is with Godot's `Array::recursive_hash` function, which will generate the same hash for any empty array regardless of the type (look at the function and you'll see what I mean). 

In `SceneState::_parse_node` all of the node's properties are looped through and if applicable added to the `variant_map` hasmap-thing:

```
prop.value = _vm_get_variant(value, variant_map);
```
In this case `value` is `bool_array` first and then `str_array`. Don't be fooled by that naming of the `_vm_get_varient` function. Here's the actual code for it:

```
static int _vm_get_variant(const Variant &p_variant, HashMap<Variant, int, VariantHasher, VariantComparator> &variant_map) {
	if (variant_map.has(p_variant)) {
		return variant_map[p_variant];
	}

	int idx = variant_map.size();
	variant_map[p_variant] = idx;
	return idx;
}
```
First it checks if `variant_map` has `p_variant`. What does this mean? It means that if _any_ variable has a value of `p_variant` already, then the other variable will get linked to that variable's value instead. I guess this is a feature of PackedScenes and isn't really the problem here.

The problem is that bool_array and str_array actually have the _same_ hashes even with different types because they are empty arrays. If we look into the `has` eventually after a long chain of calls it gets to `Array::recursive_hash` which looks like this:

```
if (recursion_count > MAX_RECURSION) {
	ERR_PRINT("Max recursion reached");
	return 0;
}

uint32_t h = hash_murmur3_one_32(Variant::ARRAY);

recursion_count++;
for (int i = 0; i < _p->array.size(); i++) {
	h = hash_murmur3_one_32(_p->array[i].recursive_hash(recursion_count), h);
}
return hash_fmix32(h);
```
So this will return the same value for any type which is causing the bug. Changing the function to this fixes the issue:

```
if (recursion_count > MAX_RECURSION) {
	ERR_PRINT("Max recursion reached");
	return 0;
}

uint32_t h = hash_murmur3_one_32(Variant::ARRAY);

if (is_empty() && get_typed_builtin() != Variant::NIL) {
	h = hash_murmur3_one_32(get_typed_builtin(), h);
}

recursion_count++;
for (int i = 0; i < _p->array.size(); i++) {
	h = hash_murmur3_one_32(_p->array[i].recursive_hash(recursion_count), h);
}
return hash_fmix32(h);
```
I see how this is similar to #72514 but the solution that would fix this issue seems to be a little different. If anyone thinks this is a good solution I would like to write a pull request for this but I'm not sure if this is the best solution to this problem ...

Also I'm not exactly sure how the assignee system works but if it's possible I would like to be assigned to this.
